<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.4.3: Using Tables to Find Function Rules</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00903</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.4.3: Using Tables to Find Function Rules</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9d4f41c8-b247-44e7-8251-bb4224155828</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>A square that has a side length of 9 cm has an area of 81 \(cm^2\). The relationship between the side length and the area of the square is a function.
    
    
    For questions a-e use the table by answering each question.
 </item>
      </list>
      <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
        <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
              <para><emphasis>Side Length \((cm)\)</emphasis></para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para><emphasis>Area \((cm^2)\)</emphasis></para>
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>
              <para>1</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>a. _______</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>2</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>b. _______</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>4</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>c. _______</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>6</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>d. _______</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(s\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
        e. _______
      </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_60bc1e49-59df-48af-9cf1-c6f24dbad542" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the area in \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(1 cm\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The area is 1 \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(1 cm\).</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 1. The area is \(1 cm^2\) when the side length is \(1 cm\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d734b03b-2f68-4d9a-ac01-c45de8b9bd8e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the area in \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(2 cm\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The area is 4 \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(2 cm\).</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 4. The area is \(4 cm^2\) when the side length is \(2 cm\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5ad92f96-7e7a-4767-824e-8d30915336d7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the area in \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(4 cm\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The area is \(16 cm^2\) when the side length is \(4 cm\).</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 16. The area is \(16 cm^2\) when the side length is \(4 cm\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_042e8ace-36ac-417f-b4a8-f3abc655352e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the area in \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(6 cm\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The area is \(36 cm^2\) when the side length is \(6 cm\).</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 36. The area is \(36 cm^2\) when the side length is \(6 cm\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write a rule for a function, \(A\), that gives the area of the square in \(cm^2\) when the side length is \(s\) \(cm\).</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(A(s)= s^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What does \(A(2)\) represent in this situation? What is its value? Write your answers, then click <emphasis>solution</emphasis> to compare your answer.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(A(2)\) is the area of the square in cm2 when the side length is 2 cm. Its value is 4.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Sketch a graph of this function.
    Use the desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course.
 </item>
      </list>
      <media alt=""><iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/></media>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your answer:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_07_06_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>A roll of paper that is 3 feet wide can be cut to any length.
       <media alt="Roll of paper, slightly unrolled. Its width is 3 feet.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_07_06_02.jpg"/>
          </media></item>
    
        
      </list>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="d950e5f9-6c9d-4634-914a-7b1c2e36f47b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>If we cut a length of 2.5 feet, what is the perimeter of the paper?
          
          
          Enter your answer in feet.<emphasis>Use integers only.</emphasis>
      </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The perimeter of the paper is 11 feet.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 11. The perimeter of the paper is 11 feet.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <para>For questions b-f, keep in mind that the roll of paper is 3 feet wide. Complete the table by answering each question.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
        <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
        side length (feet)
     </emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
        perimeter (feet)
     </emphasis> 
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>
              <para>1</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>b. _______</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>2</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>c. _______</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>6.3</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>d. _______</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>11</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>f. _______</entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_911a1006-037e-4c7a-945c-912d511b8773" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>If we cut a length of 1 foot, what is the perimeter of the paper to the nearest whole foot? 
          
      </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The perimeter is 8 feet when the side length is \(1 cm\) \((1+3+1+3=8)\).</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 8. The perimeter is 8 feet when the side length is \(1 cm) \((1+3+1+3=8)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_60456465-1eca-4c06-ab98-33262f38a76c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>To the nearest whole foot, what is the perimeter when the side length is 2 feet?
          

      </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The perimeter is 10 feet when the side length is \(2 cm\) \((2+3+2+3=10)\).</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 10. The perimeter is 10 feet when the side length is \(2 cm\) \((2+3+2+3=10)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ea0b7e59-c525-423d-9535-2e709157024e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the exact perimeter, in feet, when the side length is 6.3 feet?
          

      </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The perimeter is 18.6 feet when the side length is \(6.3 cm\) \((6.3+3+6.3+3=18.6)\).</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 18.6. The perimeter is 18.6 feet when the side length is \(6.3 cm\) \((6.3+3+6.3+3=18.6)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="eccf0aa0-7098-4211-a69a-d04113c0103d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the perimeter, to the nearest whole foot,when the side length is 11 feet?
          

      </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The perimeter is 28 feet when the side length is \(11 cm\) \((11+3+11+3=28)\).
    </para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 28. The perimeter is 28 feet when the side length is \(11 cm\) \((11+3+11+3=28)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write a rule for a function, P, that gives the perimeter of the paper in feet when the side length in feet is <emphasis>l</emphasis>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>

          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(P(l)=6+2l\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What does P(11) represent in this situation? What is its value?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>

          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(P(11)\) is the perimeter of the paper in feet when the paper is cut at a length of 11 feet. Its value is 28.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Sketch a graph of this function.
    Use the desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course.
 </item>
      </list>
      <media alt=""><iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/></media>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your answer:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_07_06_03.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <section>
      <title>Video: Writing Function Rules</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about writing rules with function notation.</para>
