        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0b0b2954ac7af8795e4139744d9614b45807b6d2"/>
      </media>
    </section>

  <note class="self-check"><para>Which function rule can describe the table below?</para>
    <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
     
      <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>
           \(x\)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
            \(y\)
         </emphasis> </entry></row>
      <row>
          <entry>
            <para>1</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>3</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            <para>2</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>5</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            <para>5</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>11</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    </note>
    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
  <section><title>Write the Function Rule from a Table</title>
    <para>Kelly can tune 6 cars in 3 hours. The table below is completed to show this relationship. The last column has the output for any \(x\). This is the rule of the function.</para>
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
      <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>
           Time spent tuning cars (\(x\))
         </emphasis> </entry><entry>
          2
          </entry>
          <entry>
          3
          </entry>
          <entry>
            6
          </entry>
          <entry>
           7
          </entry>
          <entry>
            \(x\)
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>
          Number of cars tuned up (\(y\))
          
         </emphasis> </entry><entry>
            4
          </entry>
          <entry>
           6
          </entry>
          <entry>
           12
          </entry>
          <entry>
           14
          </entry>
          <entry>
            \(2x\)
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para>Kelly can tune 6 cars in 3 hours or 2 cars in 1 hour, so the function rule is \(f(x)=2x\).</para>
    <note class="try">
    <title>Try It: Write the Function Rule from a Table</title>
    <para>Determine the rule in function notation for the table below:</para>
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
    <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>
           \(x\) (input)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry>
          1
          </entry>
          <entry>
           2
          </entry>
          <entry>
           3
          </entry>
          <entry>
            4
          </entry>
          <entry>
           \(x\)
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>
            \(y\) (output)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry>
           5
          </entry>
          <entry>
            8
          </entry>
          <entry>
            11
          </entry>
          <entry>
           14
          </entry>
          <entry>
           ?
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <exercise>
      <problem>
      
        <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>Compare your answers:</para>
      
      <para>Here is how to find the rule in function notation:</para>
      
      <para>In the table, every \(x\) is multiplied by 3, and then 2 is added. The rule is:</para>
      
      <para>\(f(x) = 3x + 2\)</para>
      
      
    
    </solution></exercise></note>
    </section>
    </section>
    
